1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,187
Chapter 32: The Newborn.

3
00:00:05,187 --> 00:00:19,637
Transition To Air Breathing Care In The Delivery Room Evaluation Of Newborn Condition Preventive Care Routine Newborn Care References In most instances at delivery, the newborn is healthy and vigorous, but at times, special care may be needed.

4
00:00:19,637 --> 00:00:33,362
For this reason, the American Academy of Pediatrics and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017a) recommend that every birth should be attended by at least one qualified individual.

5
00:00:33,362 --> 00:00:42,887
This person should be skilled in the initial steps of newborn care and positive-pressure ventilation, and their only responsibility is management of the newborn.

6
00:00:42,887 --> 00:00:51,075
This usually is a pediatrician, nurse practitioner, anesthesiologist, nurse anesthetist, or specially trained nurse.

7
00:00:51,075 --> 00:00:58,475
However, in their absence, the responsibility for neonatal resuscitation falls to the obstetrical attendant.

8
00:00:58,475 --> 00:01:03,950
Thus, obstetricians should be well versed in measures for immediate care of the newborn.

9
00:01:03,950 --> 00:01:13,337
The number and qualifications of personnel who attend the delivery will vary depending on the anticipated risk, the number of babies, and the hospital setting.

10
00:01:13,337 --> 00:01:21,787
A qualified team with full resuscitation skills should be present for high-risk deliveries and immediately available for every resuscitation.

11
00:01:21,787 --> 00:01:26,587
This team should not be on call at home or in a remote area of the hospital.

12
00:01:26,587 --> 00:01:34,137
Moreover, team training through frequent simulation practice is recommended for all who may be called to attend deliveries .

13
00:01:34,137 --> 00:01:36,612
Transition To Air Breathing.

14
00:01:36,612 --> 00:01:43,487
Immediately following birth, the newborn must promptly convert from placental to pulmonary gas exchange.

15
00:01:43,487 --> 00:01:56,037
Pulmonary vascular resistance must fall, pulmonary perfusion must rapidly rise, and unique fetal vascular shunts must begin to close to separate the systemic and pulmonary circulations.

16
00:01:56,037 --> 00:02:04,712
These shunts include the patent ductus arteriosus and patent foramen ovale, described in Chapter 7 (p. 128).

17
00:02:04,712 --> 00:02:09,312
Lung aeration is not only critical for pulmonary gas exchange.

18
00:02:09,312 --> 00:02:16,575
Specifically, recent studies suggest that it is responsible for initiating cardiovascular changes at birth .

19
00:02:16,575 --> 00:02:23,262
In utero, the fetal lungs are filled with amnionic fluid, which must be cleared quickly for air breathing.

20
00:02:23,262 --> 00:02:29,837
Various means contribute, and these mechanisms may depend on gestational age and mode of delivery.

21
00:02:29,837 --> 00:02:44,712
First, in term fetuses, a large release of fetal adrenaline late in labor stimulates pulmonary epithelial cells to stop secreting and instead to start reabsorbing lung liquid due to sodium-channel activation (te Pas, 2008).

22
00:02:44,712 --> 00:02:55,525
This mechanism is likely a minor one, as blockade of the receptors for sodium-channel activation reduces or delays but does not prevent liquid clearance from the lungs at birth .

23
00:02:55,525 --> 00:03:00,387
As a second method, mechanical forces aid lung fluid clearance.

24
00:03:00,387 --> 00:03:12,587
Early reports described expulsion of lung liquid by compression of the fetal thorax and abdomen as they passed through the birth canal (Karlberg, 1962; Saunders, 1978).

25
00:03:12,587 --> 00:03:22,387
By this mechanism, up to a third of lung liquid is expelled in a jet of fluid from the nose and mouth once the respiratory tract is exposed to the lower outside pressure.

26
00:03:22,387 --> 00:03:31,600
However, it appears that uterine contractions force a change in fetal posture, which compresses the thorax and raises intrathoracic pressures.

27
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:42,812
This expels lung liquid early in labor more so than the “vaginal squeeze” theory (Hooper, 2019; te Pas, 2008; Vyas, 1981).

28
00:03:42,812 --> 00:03:48,125
In a third mechanism, a significant amount of lung liquid is cleared after birth .

29
00:03:48,125 --> 00:03:55,362
In animal studies, most lung aeration occurs during inspiration—within three to five breaths after birth .

30
00:03:55,362 --> 00:04:02,287
The transpulmonary pressure gradient during inspiration promotes movement of fluid into the interstitial tissue.

31
00:04:02,287 --> 00:04:05,112
No liquid clears between breaths.

32
00:04:05,112 --> 00:04:12,725
From the interstitium, fluid is gradually cleared, probably by the pulmonary circulation and lymphatic vessels.

33
00:04:12,725 --> 00:04:24,912
It is possible for lung interstitial tissue pressure to rise to a point that fluid can actually move back into the airspaces during expiration unless positive end-expiratory pressure opposes liquid reentry .

34
00:04:24,912 --> 00:04:29,612
This may contribute to development of transient tachypnea of the newborn.

35
00:04:29,612 --> 00:04:31,125
As

36
00:04:31,276 --> 00:04:40,601
fluid is replaced by air, compression of the pulmonary vasculature is reduced considerably, and in turn, resistance to blood flow is lowered.

37
00:04:40,551 --> 00:04:46,913
With the fall in pulmonary arterial blood pressure, the ductus arteriosus normally closes.

38
00:04:46,913 --> 00:04:54,413
High, negative intrathoracic pressures are required to permit the initial entry of air into the fluid-filled alveoli.

39
00:04:54,413 --> 00:05:01,038
Normally, from the first breath after birth, progressively more residual air accumulates in the lung.

40
00:05:01,038 --> 00:05:06,213
With each successive breath, lower pulmonary opening pressure is required.

41
00:05:06,213 --> 00:05:15,938
In the normal mature newborn, by approximately the fifth breath, pressure-volume changes achieved with each respiration are very similar to those of the adult.

42
00:05:15,938 --> 00:05:24,388
Thus, the breathing pattern shifts from shallow episodic inspirations characteristic of the fetus to regular, deeper inhalations.

43
00:05:24,388 --> 00:05:29,776
As a last mechanism, surfactant is synthesized by type II pneumocytes.

44
00:05:29,776 --> 00:05:36,376
It lowers alveolar surface tension and helps maintain lung inflation by preventing alveolar collapse.

45
00:05:36,376 --> 00:05:43,538
Insufficient surfactant, which is common in preterm neonates, leads promptly to respiratory distress syndrome .

46
00:05:43,538 --> 00:05:49,401
In utero, umbilical venous return is the main source of preload for the left ventricle.

47
00:05:49,401 --> 00:06:01,101
Namely, the fetal pulmonary blood flow is very low due to high pulmonary vascular resistance and is unable to provide sufficient venous return to maintain left ventricular output .

48
00:06:01,101 --> 00:06:07,838
Clamping the umbilical cord reduces preload for the left ventricle and thus reduces cardiac output.

49
00:06:07,838 --> 00:06:15,726
Until the lungs aerate and pulmonary blood flow increases, the reduced cardiac output will manifest as bradycardia.

50
00:06:15,726 --> 00:06:23,463
If cord clamping is delayed until after the lungs have aerated, the transition is smoother and cardiac output does not fall .

51
00:06:23,463 --> 00:06:33,126
This understanding has led to interest in delayed (physiological) cord clamping, especially if it can be done after successful inflation of the lung.

52
00:06:33,126 --> 00:06:41,713
Specialized resuscitation trolleys have been developed to aid this practice, and several international randomized trials are underway.

53
00:06:41,713 --> 00:06:43,963
Care In The Delivery Room.

54
00:06:43,963 --> 00:06:53,288
The International Liaison Committee on Resuscitation (ILCOR) updated its scientific review for neonatal delivery room care and resuscitation .

55
00:06:53,288 --> 00:07:03,301
This review is used by the American Academy of Pediatrics and the American Heart Association to develop the neonatal resuscitation guidelines for North America .

56
00:07:03,301 --> 00:07:05,776
# Immediate Care.

57
00:07:05,776 --> 00:07:12,063
Before and during delivery, several determinants of neonatal well-being are carefully considered.

58
00:07:12,063 --> 00:07:41,426
Risk factors can include: (1) poor maternal health status; (2) prenatal complications, including any suspected fetal malformations; (3) preterm gestational age; (4) labor complications; (5) long duration of labor and ruptured membranes; (6) anesthesia that may affect placental perfusion; (7) difficult delivery; and (8) various medications given during labor and their dosages, administration routes, and timing.

59
00:07:41,426 --> 00:07:47,763
When risk factors are present, neonatal resuscitation providers should be present for the delivery.

60
00:07:47,763 --> 00:07:58,751
This team readies equipment, ensures that adequate personnel are present, delegates roles and responsibilities, and considers contingency plans to stabilize the newborn.

61
00:07:58,751 --> 00:08:12,701
In preparation, a neonatal provider will ask the delivery provider regarding expected gestational age, amnionic fluid color, additional maternal and fetal risk factors, and the management plan for umbilical cord clamping .

62
00:08:12,701 --> 00:08:17,951
Several neonatal conditions are associated with a nonvigorous presentation.

63
00:08:17,951 --> 00:08:32,088
These may include immaturity, hypoxemia or acidosis from any cause, sepsis, recent drugs administered to the mother, meconium-stained amnionic fluid, and central nervous system developmental abnormalities.

64
00:08:32,088 --> 00:08:40,863
Those related to the respiratory tract are lung abnormalities, upper airway obstruction, pneumothorax, and meconium aspiration.

65
00:08:40,863 --> 00:08:43,913
# Umbilical Cord Management.

66
00:08:43,913 --> 00:08:51,676
Obstetrical and pediatric care teams should ideally discuss plans regarding umbilical cord management prior to delivery.

67
00:08:51,676 --> 00:08:56,888
Deferred cord clamping provides transfusion of placental blood to the newborn.

68
00:08:56,888 --> 00:09:03,988
In term neonates, delaying cord clamping by 30 to 60 seconds raises hemoglobin levels at birth and

69
00:09:04,156 --> 00:09:07,293
improves iron stores during infancy.

70
00:09:07,243 --> 00:09:13,893
The only reported negative outcome is hyperbilirubinemia and a higher subsequent rate of phototherapy .

71
00:09:13,893 --> 00:09:25,081
In preterm neonates, deferred cord clamping may reduce rates of blood transfusion, intraventricular hemorrhage, and necrotizing enterocolitis and improves survival rates .

72
00:09:25,081 --> 00:09:32,156
Delayed cord clamping should be performed in preterm and term newborns who do not require resuscitation at birth.

73
00:09:32,156 --> 00:09:43,006
There should be no delay if a newborn requires resuscitation or if placental abruption, cord avulsion, or bleeding placenta previa or vasa previa complicates delivery.

74
00:09:43,006 --> 00:09:49,781
Cord milking is a reasonable alternative to immediate cord clamping for late preterm and term newborns.

75
00:09:49,781 --> 00:10:02,806
It should be avoided in neonates <28 weeks’ gestation (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020a; Gomersall, 2021; Seidler, 2021).

76
00:10:02,806 --> 00:10:05,681
# Newborn Resuscitation.

77
00:10:05,681 --> 00:10:14,781
Approximately 10 percent of newborns require some degree of active resuscitation to stimulate breathing, and 1 percent need extensive care.

78
00:10:14,781 --> 00:10:26,018
Perhaps not coincidentally, the neonatal death rate with home birth in the United States is small but still fourfold greater than attended hospital births (Grünebaum, 2020).

79
00:10:26,018 --> 00:10:34,993
When deprived of adequate gas exchange, either before or after birth, neonates demonstrate a well-defined sequence of events leading to apnea .

80
00:10:34,993 --> 00:10:47,256
With oxygen deprivation and carbon dioxide (CO2) elevation, there is a transient period of rapid breathing, and if it persists, breathing stops, which is termed primary apnea.

81
00:10:47,256 --> 00:10:52,518
This stage is accompanied by a fall in heart rate and loss of neuromuscular tone.

82
00:10:52,518 --> 00:10:56,781
Simple stimulation will usually reverse primary apnea.

83
00:10:56,781 --> 00:11:06,131
If oxygen deprivation and asphyxia persist, however, the newborn will develop deep gasping respirations, followed by secondary apnea.

84
00:11:06,131 --> 00:11:13,893
This latter stage is associated with a further decline in heart rate, fall in blood pressure, and loss of neuromuscular tone.

85
00:11:13,893 --> 00:11:21,431
Neonates in secondary apnea will not respond to stimulation and will not spontaneously resume respiratory efforts.

86
00:11:21,431 --> 00:11:25,131
Unless ventilation is assisted, death follows.

87
00:11:25,131 --> 00:11:27,693
Figure 32-1.

88
00:11:27,693 --> 00:11:33,693
Physiological changes associated with primary and secondary apnea in the newborn.

89
00:11:33,693 --> 00:11:40,443
bpm = beats per minute; HR = heart rate; MAP = mean arterial pressure.

90
00:11:40,443 --> 00:11:53,568
Reproduced with permission from Kattwinkel J: Textbook of Neonatal Resuscitation, 6th ed. Elk Grove Village, American Academy of Pediatrics and American Heart Association, 2010.

91
00:11:53,568 --> 00:11:58,943
Clinically, primary and secondary apneas are initially indistinguishable.

92
00:11:58,943 --> 00:12:07,218
When a response to stimulation is not immediate, resuscitation with effective ventilation of the apneic newborn must begin quickly.

93
00:12:07,218 --> 00:12:13,618
Effective positivepressure ventilation is the most critical intervention in neonatal resuscitation.

94
00:12:13,618 --> 00:12:16,568
# Resuscitation Protocol.

95
00:12:16,568 --> 00:12:26,906
Initial Assessment Immediately after birth and usually during the delay for umbilical cord clamping, newborn tone, respiratory effort, and heart rate are evaluated .

96
00:12:26,906 --> 00:12:31,618
Most term neonates are vigorous by 10 to 30 seconds after birth .

97
00:12:31,618 --> 00:12:37,543
For these, initial steps of warming the newborn can be done on the mother’s chest or abdomen.

98
00:12:37,543 --> 00:12:49,231
Direct skin-to-skin contact with the mother and drying and covering the newborn with a warm blanket will help maintain euthermia (36.5 to 37.5°C).

99
00:12:49,231 --> 00:12:57,606
A vigorously crying newborn does not require routine oral suctioning (Aziz 2020; Foster, 2017).

100
00:12:57,606 --> 00:13:07,006
Instead, bulb suctioning to remove secretions is best reserved for those who cannot clear secretions on their own due to apnea or copious secretions.

101
00:13:07,006 --> 00:13:16,643
Additional routine care steps include drying, gentle stimulation by rubbing the newborn’s back, and continued observation during the transition period.

102
00:13:16,643 --> 00:13:19,168
Figure 32-2.

103
00:13:19,168 --> 00:13:35,131
Algorithm for resuscitation of the newborn based on the International Liaison Committee on Resuscitation scientific review and recommended by the American Academy of Pediatrics and American Heart Association (Aziz, 2020; Wyckoff, 2020).

104
00:13:35,131 --> 00:13:54,768
bpm = beats per minute; CPAP = continuous positive airway pressure; ECG = electrocardiogram; ETT = endotracheal tube; HR = heart rate; IV = intravenous; PPV = positive-pressure ventilation; SpO2 = peripheral oxygen

105
00:13:54,940 --> 00:13:59,565
saturation; UVC = umbilical venous catheter.

106
00:13:59,515 --> 00:14:07,215
If not vigorous or if preterm, the neonate is carried to a prewarmed radiant warmer for initial newborn care steps.

107
00:14:07,215 --> 00:14:11,677
The initial wet birth blanket is removed to allow newborn drying.

108
00:14:11,677 --> 00:14:16,977
Cold stress is associated with multiple neonatal morbidities and mortality.

109
00:14:16,977 --> 00:14:23,265
Preterm infants are particularly vulnerable, and special steps can help maintain euthermia.

110
00:14:23,265 --> 00:14:50,840
These are providing a warm delivery room (>25°C), covering the neonatal head with either a plastic or wool hat, applying polyethylene plastic “ponchos” or wraps to slow evaporative heat losses, using chemically activated thermal mattresses to reduce conductive heat loss, and administering warm, humidified respiratory gases during respiratory stabilization (Aziz, 2020; Wyckoff, 2020).

111
00:14:50,840 --> 00:14:57,402
At the radiant warmer, newborns are positioned with mild neck extension to maximally open the airway.

112
00:14:57,402 --> 00:15:06,615
If the newborn is apneic or has copious secretions that obstruct the airway, then a bulb syringe or suction catheter can help clear the mouth and the nose.

113
00:15:06,615 --> 00:15:20,365
Routine intubation and suctioning of meconium-stained amnionic fluid is not recommended for the nonvigorous newborn (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2021a; Wyckoff, 2020).

114
00:15:20,365 --> 00:15:24,977
Intubation and suction are reserved for suspected airway obstruction.

115
00:15:24,977 --> 00:15:31,627
Meconium-stained amnionic fluid is a significant risk factor for the need for advanced resuscitation.

116
00:15:31,627 --> 00:15:43,452
In rare circumstances, the meconiumstained fluid can obstruct the newborn’s airway and necessitate intubation and tracheal suctioning (Aziz, 2020; Wyckoff, 2020).

117
00:15:43,452 --> 00:15:59,040
After completion of the initial stabilization steps, persisting apnea, gasping respirations, or heart rate ≤100 beats per minute (bpm) should prompt immediate administration of positive-pressure ventilation with room air .

118
00:15:59,040 --> 00:16:05,540
This should be started by 60 seconds of life, if not sooner, once the initial steps are completed.

119
00:16:05,540 --> 00:16:08,165
Figure 32-3.

120
00:16:08,165 --> 00:16:11,365
Correct use of bag-and-mask ventilation.

121
00:16:11,365 --> 00:16:16,377
The head should be in a sniffing position with the tip of the nose pointing to the ceiling.

122
00:16:16,377 --> 00:16:19,265
The neck should not be hyperextended.

123
00:16:19,265 --> 00:16:28,252
Mask Ventilation Assisted ventilation by facemask at a rate of 40 to 60 breaths per minute is recommended as an initial resuscitation step.

124
00:16:28,252 --> 00:16:32,402
Oxygen saturation is monitored by pulse oximetry.

125
00:16:32,402 --> 00:16:41,227
Supplemental oxygen can be given in graduated, rising percentages to maintain oxygen saturation values within a normal range.

126
00:16:41,227 --> 00:16:45,640
Percentage goals rise progressively with accruing minutes of life .

127
00:16:45,640 --> 00:16:50,127
Adequate ventilation is best indicated by an improved heart rate.

128
00:16:50,127 --> 00:17:03,690
A colorimetric end-tidal carbon dioxide (ETCO2) monitor that lies between the positive-pressure device and facemask serves as a helpful adjunct to detect successful gas exchange during mask ventilation.

129
00:17:03,690 --> 00:17:06,302
Table 32-1.

130
00:17:06,302 --> 00:17:20,790
Oxygen Saturation Goals per Minute of Life If the heart rate remains ≤100 beats per minute after 5 to 10 positive pressure breaths, and the attempted ventilation is inadequate, then further corrective steps should begin.

131
00:17:20,790 --> 00:17:24,327
These can be remembered by the mnemonic MR. SOPA .

132
00:17:24,327 --> 00:17:32,652
The two most common problems are mask leak due to an ineffective seal and malposition of the airway (Schmölzer, 2019).

133
00:17:32,652 --> 00:17:36,127
Mask positioning should maintain an open mouth.

134
00:17:36,127 --> 00:17:41,640
This provides a superior conduit for gas transfer than the narrow-caliber nares.

135
00:17:41,640 --> 00:17:50,765
If the other corrective steps do not raise the heart rate, either intubation with an endotracheal tube or placement of a supraglottic airway is required.

136
00:17:50,765 --> 00:17:53,302
Table 32-2.

137
00:17:53,302 --> 00:18:02,927
Ventilation Corrective Steps (MR. SOPA) Alternative Airway If mask ventilation is ineffective or prolonged, an alternative airway is placed.

138
00:18:02,927 --> 00:18:12,265
For tracheal intubation, a laryngoscope with a straight blade—size 0 for a preterm newborn and size 1 for a term neonate—is used.

139
00:18:12,265 --> 00:18:15,302
Gentle cricoid pressure may be useful.

140
00:18:15,302 --> 00:18:24,752
An increasing heart rate and ETCO2 detection after several breaths are the primary methods to confirm intubation of the trachea and not the esophagus.

141
00:18:24,752 --> 00:18:35,877
One can also look for symmetrical chest wall motion; auscultate for equal breath sounds, especially in the axillae; and auscultate for the absence of breath sounds or gurgling over the

142
00:18:36,028 --> 00:18:37,615
stomach.

143
00:18:37,565 --> 00:18:44,028
Once in place, the tube is used for tracheal suctioning only for a suspected obstructed airway.

144
00:18:44,028 --> 00:18:54,265
Otherwise, an appropriate positive-pressure device (self-inflating bag, flow-inflating bag, or T-piece resuscitator) is attached to the endotracheal tube .

145
00:18:54,265 --> 00:19:01,315
Ventilation breaths are delivered at a rate of 40 to 60 per minute with a force adequate to stabilize the heart rate.

146
00:19:01,315 --> 00:19:10,053
In term newborns, opening pressures of 30 to 40 cm H2O typically will expand the alveoli without causing barotrauma.

147
00:19:10,053 --> 00:19:17,215
Once the lung is inflated, less pressure is typically needed (20 to 25 cm H2O).

148
00:19:17,215 --> 00:19:23,990
For preterm neonates, pressures of 20 to 25 cm H2O are typically used.

149
00:19:23,990 --> 00:19:32,728
Increases in heart rate and peripheral oxygen saturation (SpO2) levels to acceptable ranges reflect a positive response.

150
00:19:32,728 --> 00:19:40,403
Chest Compressions Most commonly, effective ventilation is all that is required to stabilize the newborn in the delivery room.

151
00:19:40,403 --> 00:19:50,953
If the heart rate remains <60 bpm despite ventilation corrective steps, including placement of an endotracheal tube, chest compressions are initiated.

152
00:19:50,953 --> 00:20:01,478
Once the endotracheal tube is secured, compressions are done from the head of the bed rather than the side so that space is opened up for a provider to have umbilical venous access.

153
00:20:01,478 --> 00:20:07,665
When compressions are initiated, the oxygen concentration is increased to 100 percent.

154
00:20:07,665 --> 00:20:13,665
With the two-thumb compression method, hands encircle the chest, while the thumbs depress the sternum.

155
00:20:13,665 --> 00:20:20,040
Compressions are delivered on the lower third of the sternum at a depth sufficient to generate a palpable pulse.

156
00:20:20,040 --> 00:20:25,153
This is typically one third of the anterior-posterior diameter of the chest.

157
00:20:25,153 --> 00:20:36,815
Compared with other techniques, this method offers less provider fatigue over time, yields higher generated perfusion pressures, and lessens hand malpositioning that could cause traumatic injury .

158
00:20:36,815 --> 00:20:46,628
A 3:1 compressions-to-ventilation ratio is recommended, and 90 compressions and 30 breaths achieve approximately 120 events each minute.

159
00:20:46,628 --> 00:20:55,228
Coordinated chest compressions and ventilations should continue until the spontaneous heart rate is ≥60 bpm .

160
00:20:55,228 --> 00:21:05,328
If the heart rate remains ≤60 bpm after adequate ventilation and chest compressions, intravenously administered epinephrine is indicated.

161
00:21:05,328 --> 00:21:12,528
The recommended intravenous dose is 0.01 to 0.03 mg/kg.

162
00:21:12,528 --> 00:21:20,453
Epinephrine may be given through the endotracheal tube if venous access has not been established, but its action is less reliable .

163
00:21:20,453 --> 00:21:29,390
If given through the endotracheal tube, higher doses are employed—0.05 to 0.1 mg/kg.

164
00:21:29,390 --> 00:21:42,965
Resuscitation Discontinuation ILCOR concludes that it is reasonable to discontinue resuscitative efforts for a neonate who remains without a heartbeat despite at least 20 minutes of continuous and adequate resuscitative efforts .

165
00:21:42,965 --> 00:21:49,565
Notably, the decision to continue or discontinue resuscitative efforts must be individualized.

166
00:21:49,565 --> 00:22:00,378
The time was recently lengthened due to reports of some newborns enrolled in therapeutic hypothermia trials who had a 10-minute Apgar score of 0 yet survived without disability.

167
00:22:00,378 --> 00:22:05,540
This suggests the need for caution in limiting resuscitation to 10 minutes .

168
00:22:05,540 --> 00:22:08,490
Evaluation Of Newborn Condition.

169
00:22:08,490 --> 00:22:10,890
# Apgar Score.

170
00:22:10,890 --> 00:22:23,553
The scoring system described by Dr. Virginia Apgar in 1953 remains a useful clinical tool to classify newborn health immediately after birth and to assess the effectiveness of resuscitative measures .

171
00:22:23,553 --> 00:22:37,078
During scoring, each of five easily identifiable characteristics—heart rate, respiratory effort, muscle tone, reflex irritability, and color—is assessed and assigned a value of 0, 1, or 2 .

172
00:22:37,078 --> 00:22:44,478
In the currently recommended expanded form, concurrent resuscitation interventions also are recorded over time.

173
00:22:44,478 --> 00:22:52,203
The total score, based on the sum of the five components, is determined in all neonates at 1 and 5 minutes after delivery.

174
00:22:52,203 --> 00:23:02,390
In those with a score <7, the score may be calculated at further 5minute intervals until a 20-minute Apgar score is assigned or resuscitation efforts are halted .

175
00:23:02,390 --> 00:23:05,015
Table 32-3.

176
00:23:05,015 --> 00:23:18,828
20-Minute Expanded Apgar Score In an analysis of more than 150,000 newborns delivered at Parkland Hospital, Casey and associates (2001b) assessed the significance of the 5minute score

177
00:23:18,988 --> 00:23:23,200
for predicting survival during the first 28 days of life.

178
00:23:23,150 --> 00:23:32,038
They found that in term neonates, the risk of neonatal death was approximately 1 in 5000 for those with Apgar scores of 7 to 10.

179
00:23:32,038 --> 00:23:40,150
This risk compares with a mortality rate of 25 percent for term newborns with 5-minute scores ≤3.

180
00:23:40,150 --> 00:23:46,000
Low 5-minute scores were comparably predictive of neonatal death in preterm neonates.

181
00:23:46,000 --> 00:24:03,038
Similarly, an analysis of more than 113,000 preterm neonates ranging from 22 to 36 weeks’ gestation found that Apgar scores at 5 and 10 minutes provided prognostic information for neonatal survival rates across gestational-age strata .

182
00:24:03,038 --> 00:24:09,163
Thus, the Apgar scoring system remains relevant for the prediction of neonatal survival.

183
00:24:09,163 --> 00:24:15,325
Apgar scores alone are not predictive of hypoxic ischemic encephalopathy (HIE).

184
00:24:15,325 --> 00:24:29,238
However, in cohorts of babies diagnosed with HIE based on umbilical cord blood pH and standardized neurologic examinations, low 10-minute Apgar scores are significantly associated with school-age outcomes .

185
00:24:29,238 --> 00:24:36,638
Previously, many groups established erroneous definitions of asphyxia based solely on low Apgar scores.

186
00:24:36,638 --> 00:24:51,238
These prompted the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists and American Academy of Pediatrics (2021b) to issue a series of joint opinions with important caveats regarding Apgar score limitations.

187
00:24:51,238 --> 00:25:02,725
Certain elements of the Apgar score are partially dependent on the physiological maturity of the newborn, and a healthy, preterm neonate may receive a low score only because of immaturity.

188
00:25:02,725 --> 00:25:09,388
Other influencing factors include fetal malformations, maternal medications, and infection.

189
00:25:09,388 --> 00:25:14,925
Thus, it is inappropriate to use an Apgar score alone to diagnose asphyxia.

190
00:25:14,925 --> 00:25:25,063
Moreover, the Apgar score alone cannot establish hypoxia as the cause of cerebral palsy, as discussed in Chapter 33 (p. 604).

191
00:25:25,063 --> 00:25:29,063
# Umbilical Cord Blood Acid–Base Studies.

192
00:25:29,063 --> 00:25:36,450
Blood taken from umbilical vessels may be used for acid–base studies to assess the metabolic status of the neonate.

193
00:25:36,450 --> 00:25:48,600
Blood collection is performed following delivery by immediately isolating a 10- to 20-cm segment of cord with two clamps placed near the neonate and another two clamps positioned nearer the placenta.

194
00:25:48,600 --> 00:25:53,988
The cord is then cut between the two proximal clamps and then the two distal clamps .

195
00:25:53,988 --> 00:26:15,663
Arterial blood is drawn from the isolated cord segment into a 1- to 2-mL commercially prepared plastic syringe containing lyophilized heparin or a similar syringe that has been flushed with a heparin solution containing 1000 U/mL. Once sampling is completed, the needle is capped and the syringe transported, on ice, to the laboratory.

196
00:26:15,663 --> 00:26:28,725
Although efforts should be made for prompt transport, neither the pH nor partial pressure of CO2 (Pco2) values change significantly in blood kept at room temperature for up to 60 minutes .

197
00:26:28,725 --> 00:26:40,163
Mathematical models have been developed that allow reasonable prediction of birth acid–base status in properly collected cord blood samples analyzed as late as 60 hours after delivery .

198
00:26:40,163 --> 00:26:47,125
Notably, acid–base measurements can show significant variances between different analyzing devices .

199
00:26:47,125 --> 00:26:50,525
# Fetal Acid–Base Physiology.

200
00:26:50,525 --> 00:26:54,750
The fetus produces both carbonic and organic acids.

201
00:26:54,750 --> 00:27:01,250
Carbonic acid (H2CO3) forms from oxidative metabolism of CO2.

202
00:27:01,250 --> 00:27:06,425
The fetus usually rapidly clears CO2 through the placental circulation.

203
00:27:06,425 --> 00:27:11,075
If CO2 clearance drops, carbonic acid levels rise.

204
00:27:11,075 --> 00:27:14,750
This often follows impaired placental exchange.

205
00:27:14,750 --> 00:27:23,925
When H2CO3 accumulates in fetal blood and organic acids do not concurrently rise, the result is respiratory acidemia.

206
00:27:23,925 --> 00:27:30,675
In contrast, organic acids primarily include lactic and β-hydroxybutyric acids.

207
00:27:30,675 --> 00:27:38,050
Levels of these increase with persistent placental exchange impairment, and they result from anaerobic glycolysis.

208
00:27:38,050 --> 00:27:42,175
These organic acids are cleared slowly from fetal blood.

209
00:27:42,175 --> 00:27:49,663
When they accumulate, without a concurrent increase in H2CO3, the result is metabolic acidemia.

210
00:27:49,663 --> 00:27:58,875
With the development of metabolic acidemia, bicarbonate (HCO3−) levels drop because it is used to buffer the organic acid.

211
00:27:58,875 --> 00:28:09,700
A rise in H2CO3 concentrations accompanied by greater organic acid levels, reflected by decreased HCO3− levels, causes mixed

212
00:28:09,868 --> 00:28:12,880
respiratory-metabolic acidemia.

213
00:28:12,830 --> 00:28:22,118
In the fetus, respiratory and metabolic acidemia and ultimately tissue acidosis are most likely part of a progressively worsening continuum.

214
00:28:22,118 --> 00:28:35,693
This is different from adult pathophysiology, in which distinct conditions result either in respiratory acidosis—for example, pulmonary disease, or in metabolic acidosis—for example, diabetes.

215
00:28:35,693 --> 00:28:41,880
In the fetus, the placenta serves as both the lungs and, to a certain degree, the kidneys.

216
00:28:41,880 --> 00:28:47,355
One principal cause of fetal acidemia is a drop in uteroplacental perfusion.

217
00:28:47,355 --> 00:28:57,455
This creates retention of CO2, that is, respiratory acidemia, and if protracted and severe enough, yields a mixed or metabolic acidemia.

218
00:28:57,455 --> 00:29:09,843
Assuming that maternal pH and blood gases are normal, the actual pH of fetal blood depends on the proportion of carbonic and organic acids and the amount of bicarbonate, which is the major buffer in blood.

219
00:29:09,843 --> 00:29:21,855
This can best be illustrated by the Henderson–Hasselbalch equation: For clinical purposes, HCO3− represents the metabolic component and is reported in mEq/L.

220
00:29:21,855 --> 00:29:30,218
The H2CO3 concentration reflects the respiratory component and is reported as the Pco2 in mm Hg.

221
00:29:30,218 --> 00:29:34,393
Thus: The result of this equation is a pH value.

222
00:29:34,393 --> 00:29:40,705
Because pH is a logarithmic term, it does not give a linear measure of acid accumulation.

223
00:29:40,705 --> 00:29:54,605
For example, a change in hydrogen ion concentration associated with a fall in pH from 7.0 to 6.9 is almost twice that which is associated with a fall in pH from 7.3 to 7.2.

224
00:29:54,605 --> 00:30:03,355
For this reason, the change in base—termed delta base—offers a more linear measure of the degree of accumulation of metabolic acid .

225
00:30:03,355 --> 00:30:11,405
The delta base is a calculated number used as a measure of the change in buffering capacity of bicarbonate (HCO3−).

226
00:30:11,405 --> 00:30:22,768
The formula for calculating the base excess (BE) is as follows: Shown in Figure 32-4 is a nomogram from which these can be calculated if only two parameters are known.

227
00:30:22,768 --> 00:30:31,780
For example, the HCO3− concentration declines with a metabolic acidemia as it is consumed to maintain a normal pH.

228
00:30:31,780 --> 00:30:42,580
A base deficit develops when the HCO3− concentration drops below normal levels, and a base excess occurs when HCO3− values are above normal.

229
00:30:42,580 --> 00:31:01,505
Importantly, a mixed respiratory–metabolic acidemia with a large base deficit and a low HCO3−, for example 12 mmol/L, is more often associated with a depressed neonate than is a mixed acidemia with a minimal base deficit and a more nearly normal HCO3− level.

