235
00:31:20,630 --> 00:31:24,230
# Clinical Significance of Acidemia.

236
00:31:24,230 --> 00:31:29,580
Fetal oxygenation and pH generally decline during the course of normal labor.

237
00:31:29,580 --> 00:31:38,068
Normal umbilical cord blood pH and blood gas values at delivery in term newborns are summarized in Table 32-4.

238
00:31:38,068 --> 00:31:46,130
Similar values have been reported for preterm neonates (Ramin, 1989; Skiold, 2017).

239
00:31:46,130 --> 00:31:59,318
The lower limits of normal pH in the newborn range from 7.05 to 7.16 for newborns 28 to 42 weeks’ gestation at birth, with some variation based on gestational age .

240
00:31:59,318 --> 00:32:04,530
Thus, these values should be considered to define neonatal acidemia.

241
00:32:04,530 --> 00:32:20,680
Even so, most fetuses will tolerate intrapartum acidemia with a pH as low as 7.00 without incurring neurological impairment (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2014; Lee, 2020).

242
00:32:20,680 --> 00:32:37,968
That said, in a study of newborns with a pH <7.0 from Parkland Hospital, the incidences of neonatal deaths—8 percent, intensive care admissions—39 percent, intubations—14 percent, and seizures—13 percent, were notable .

243
00:32:37,968 --> 00:32:48,355
In a study of more than 51,000 term newborns, the incidence of neonatal encephalopathy in those with a birth pH <7.0 was 3 percent .

244
00:32:48,355 --> 00:32:58,293
Even those who had normal 5-minute Apgar scores but who had an arterial cord pH values <7.0 experienced a higher risk of morbidity.

245
00:32:58,293 --> 00:33:04,780
This included respiratory distress, neonatal intensive care unit admission, and sepsis .

246
00:33:04,780 --> 00:33:10,093
Faster acidemia resolution after birth is associated with better outcome .

247
00:33:10,093 --> 00:33:12,718
Table 32-4.

248
00:33:12,718 --> 00:33:18,680
Umbilical Cord Blood pH and Blood Gas Values in Normal Term Newborns Respiratory

249
00:33:18,844 --> 00:33:27,719
Acidemia Acute interruption in placental gas exchange is accompanied by subsequent CO2 retention and respiratory acidemia.

250
00:33:27,669 --> 00:33:32,931
The most common antecedent factor is transient umbilical cord compression.

251
00:33:32,931 --> 00:33:37,669
Generally, respiratory acidemia is not harmful to the fetus .

252
00:33:37,669 --> 00:33:45,581
The degree to which pH is affected by Pco2—the respiratory component of the acidosis—can be calculated.

253
00:33:45,581 --> 00:33:55,294
First, the upper normal neonatal Pco2 value of about 50 mm Hg is subtracted from the cord blood gas Pco2 value.

254
00:33:55,294 --> 00:34:02,994
Each additional 10 mm Hg Pco2 increment will lower the pH by 0.08 units .

255
00:34:02,994 --> 00:34:10,369
Thus, in a mixed respiratory–metabolic acidemia, the benign respiratory component can be calculated.

256
00:34:10,369 --> 00:34:17,656
As an example, acute cord prolapse during labor prompts cesarean delivery of a neonate 20 minutes later.

257
00:34:17,656 --> 00:34:25,256
The umbilical artery blood gas pH was 6.95 and the Pco2 was 90 mm Hg.

258
00:34:25,256 --> 00:34:35,581
The degree to which the cord compression and subsequent impairment of CO2 exchange affected the pH is calculated using the relationship given earlier and shown below.

259
00:34:35,581 --> 00:34:42,669
Thus, the pH before cord prolapse was approximately 7.27, well within normal limits.

260
00:34:42,669 --> 00:34:47,219
Thus, the low pH resulted from respiratory acidosis.

261
00:34:47,219 --> 00:34:59,206
Metabolic Acidemia The fetus begins to develop metabolic acidemia when oxygen deprivation is sufficiently long and severe to require anaerobic metabolism for cellular energy needs.

262
00:34:59,206 --> 00:35:14,269
Low and associates (1997) defined fetal acidosis as a base deficit ≥12 mmol/L, and severe fetal acidosis as a base deficit ≥16 mmol/L.

263
00:35:14,269 --> 00:35:26,881
In the Parkland study of more than 150,000 newborns cited earlier, metabolic acidemia was defined using umbilical cord blood gas thresholds that were two standard deviations below the mean .

264
00:35:26,881 --> 00:35:53,531
Thus, metabolic acidemia was an umbilical artery blood pH <7.00 accompanied by a Pco2 ≤76.3 mm Hg, with higher values indicating a respiratory component; HCO3− concentration ≤17.7 mmol/L; and base deficit ≥10.3 mEq/L.

265
00:35:53,531 --> 00:36:16,281
From the standpoint of possible neurological injury, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2014) defines metabolic acidosis as umbilical arterial pH <7.0 and a base deficit ≥12 mmol/L. Metabolic acidemia is associated with a high rate of multiorgan dysfunction.

266
00:36:16,281 --> 00:36:25,581
In rare cases, such hypoxia-induced metabolic acidemia may be so severe that it causes subsequent hypoxic ischemic encephalopathy .

267
00:36:25,581 --> 00:36:32,719
Severe metabolic acidosis is poorly predictive of subsequent neurological impairment in the term neonate .

268
00:36:32,719 --> 00:36:40,031
In fact, a fetus without such acidemia cannot by definition have suffered recent hypoxicinduced injury.

269
00:36:40,031 --> 00:36:55,794
In preterm infants, newborn acid–base status is more closely linked to intraventricular hemorrhage, periventricular leukomalacia, and poorer long-term neurological outcomes (Baalbaki, 2021; Morgan, 2017).

270
00:36:55,794 --> 00:37:04,906
Associations between metabolic acidemia, low Apgar scores, and neonatal death have been described in term and preterm newborns .

271
00:37:04,906 --> 00:37:20,706
Regarding term neonates, the risk of neonatal death was more than 3200-fold greater in term neonates with metabolic acidemia and 5-minute scores ≤3 compared with those with a 5-minute Apgar score ≥7 .

272
00:37:20,706 --> 00:37:25,144
# Recommendations for Cord Blood Gas Determinations.

273
00:37:25,144 --> 00:37:34,506
In some centers, cord gas analysis is performed in all neonates at birth (Morgan, 2015; Sabol, 2016).

274
00:37:34,506 --> 00:37:42,019
Cost-effectiveness analysis for universal cord blood gas measurement suggests benefit and potential cost savings .

275
00:37:42,019 --> 00:37:55,206
It seems reasonable to obtain cord blood gas determinations for intrapartum cases of cesarean delivery for fetal compromise, abnormal fetal heart rate tracing, fever, and low 5-minute Apgar score.

276
00:37:55,206 --> 00:38:00,206
Multifetal gestation and severely growth-restricted fetuses are others.

277
00:38:00,206 --> 00:38:13,344
Although umbilical cord acid–base blood determinations are poorly predictive of either immediate or long-term adverse neurological outcome, they provide the most objective evidence of the fetal metabolic status at birth.

278
00:38:13,344 --> 00:38:15,219
Preventive Care.

279
00:38:15,219 --> 00:38:18,331
# Eye Infection Prophylaxis.

280
00:38:18,331 --> 00:38:23,319
Ophthalmia neonatorum is mucopurulent conjunctivitis of newborns.

281
00:38:23,319 --> 00:38:28,219
Gonococcal and chlamydial infections are among the most common causes .

282
00:38:28,219 --> 00:38:34,406
Neisseria gonorrhoeae infection acquired at birth was a common cause of childhood blindness in the

283
00:38:34,564 --> 00:38:36,089
past.

284
00:38:36,039 --> 00:38:41,301
Gonococcal prophylaxis is now mandatory for all neonates in most states .

285
00:38:41,301 --> 00:38:52,576
For prophylaxis soon after delivery, 0.5-percent erythromycin ophthalmic ointment is recommended (U.S. Preventive Services Task Force, 2019).

286
00:38:52,576 --> 00:39:11,489
For a neonate born to a mother with untreated gonorrhea, treatment of presumptive neonatal gonococcal conjunctivitis is a single ceftriaxone dose, 25 to 50 mg/kg, intravenously or intramuscularly, not to exceed 125 mg intramuscularly .

287
00:39:11,489 --> 00:39:18,076
Before treatment, neonatal testing for both gonococcal and chlamydia infections should be obtained.

288
00:39:18,076 --> 00:39:23,626
With chlamydial conjunctivitis, adequate neonatal prophylaxis is challenging.

289
00:39:23,626 --> 00:39:30,601
Ideally, prenatal screening and treatment for Chlamydia trachomatis obviates conjunctival infection .

290
00:39:30,601 --> 00:39:38,776
In neonates delivered vaginally of mothers with an active chlamydial infection, 30 to 50 percent will develop conjunctivitis .

291
00:39:38,776 --> 00:39:45,339
Prophylactic topical eye treatments do not reliably reduce the incidence of chlamydial conjunctivitis.

292
00:39:45,339 --> 00:39:51,851
Conjunctivitis in a newborn up to age 3 months should prompt consideration for chlamydial infection .

293
00:39:51,851 --> 00:40:04,401
Treatment for neonatal chlamydial conjunctivitis is oral erythromycin base or ethylsuccinate, 50 mg/kg/d given daily and divided into four doses for 14 days.

294
00:40:04,401 --> 00:40:11,651
Another is oral azithromycin, 20 mg/kg given as a single daily dose for 3 days.

295
00:40:11,651 --> 00:40:18,726
A second course may be required since the efficacy of erythromycin therapy approximates only 80 percent.

296
00:40:18,726 --> 00:40:22,526
Posttreatment follow-up of these infants is recommended .

297
00:40:22,526 --> 00:40:25,664
# Hepatitis B Immunization.

298
00:40:25,664 --> 00:40:38,126
Routine immunization with thimerosal-free vaccine against hepatitis B within the first 24 hours of life is standard practice for all medically stable newborns with birthweights >2000 g .

299
00:40:38,126 --> 00:40:46,964
If the mother is seropositive for hepatitis B surface antigen, the neonate is also passively immunized with hepatitis B immune globulin.

300
00:40:46,964 --> 00:40:57,989
For high-risk or even all seropositive women during pregnancy, some advocate treatment with antiviral nucleoside or nucleotide analogues to minimize fetal transmission .

301
00:40:57,989 --> 00:41:05,426
# Vitamin K. Supplemental vitamin K injection will prevent vitamin K deficiency bleeding of the newborn .

302
00:41:05,426 --> 00:41:13,264
A single intramuscular dose of vitamin K, 0.5 to 1 mg, is given within 1 hour of birth .

303
00:41:13,264 --> 00:41:15,739
# COVID-19.

304
00:41:15,739 --> 00:41:24,351
The novel SARS-CoV-2 virus is very contagious, spreads person to person, and can cause severe morbidity or mortality.

305
00:41:24,351 --> 00:41:36,776
Thus, birth attendants at the delivery of a mother with COVID-19 should implement airborne and contact isolation precautions to protect themselves during potentially aerosolizing resuscitation procedures .

306
00:41:36,776 --> 00:41:49,176
National data suggest that approximately 2 percent of newborns of women who test positive for SARS-CoV-2 near delivery have also tested positive in the first 24 to 96 hours of life .

307
00:41:49,176 --> 00:41:56,376
Newborn death directly attributable to perinatal infection with SARS-CoV-2 is rare in the United States.

308
00:41:56,376 --> 00:42:06,126
However, evidence suggests that symptomatic maternal infection at delivery is associated with increased risks of preterm birth and perinatal morbidity .

309
00:42:06,126 --> 00:42:10,101
Infected mothers should wear a mask while holding their newborn.

310
00:42:10,101 --> 00:42:18,539
A mother who is acutely ill with COVID-19 may be unable to care for her neonate and may need temporary separation until she improves.

311
00:42:18,539 --> 00:42:27,039
Mothers who are infected but who are not acutely ill can room-in with their well newborn but should maintain a reasonable distance from their babies when possible.

312
00:42:27,039 --> 00:42:31,939
Breastfeeding while wearing a mask and maintaining good hand hygiene is suitable .

313
00:42:31,939 --> 00:42:37,464
The newborn should be tested at least once for SARS-CoV-2 prior to discharge .

314
00:42:37,464 --> 00:42:40,001
# Newborn Screening.

315
00:42:40,001 --> 00:42:51,301
Mass-screening tests are available for 35 newborn conditions, and these are recommended by the Advisory Committee on Heritable Disorders in Newborns and Children (2018).

316
00:42:51,301 --> 00:42:57,114
Shown in Table 32-5, many are mandated by various state laws.

317
00:42:57,114 --> 00:43:01,701
Most states require that all tests in the core panel be performed.

318
00:43:01,701 --> 00:43:07,964
Supplemental conditions—secondary conditions—are listed on the Advisory Committee’s website.

319
00:43:07,964 --> 00:43:12,864
Some states require some of these in addition to their mandated core panel.

320
00:43:12,864 --> 00:43:19,164
Each practitioner should be familiar with their individual state requirements, which are available at:

321
00:43:19,324 --> 00:43:24,161
babysfirsttest.org/newborn-screening/states.

322
00:43:24,111 --> 00:43:26,811
Table 32-5.

323
00:43:26,811 --> 00:43:30,611
Newborn Screening Core Panel Routine Newborn Care.

324
00:43:30,611 --> 00:43:33,811
# Gestational Age Estimation.

325
00:43:33,811 --> 00:43:38,636
Newborn gestational age can be estimated very soon after delivery.

326
00:43:38,636 --> 00:43:45,399
The relationship between gestational age and birthweight can identify neonates at risk for complications.

327
00:43:45,399 --> 00:43:57,586
For example, neonates who are either small or large for gestational age are at greater risk for hypoglycemia and polycythemia, and measurements of blood glucose and hematocrit are indicated.

328
00:43:57,586 --> 00:44:00,961
# Care of Skin and Umbilical Cord.

329
00:44:00,961 --> 00:44:08,099
All excess vernix, blood, and meconium is gently wiped off after delivery while keeping the newborn warm.

330
00:44:08,099 --> 00:44:13,624
Any remaining vernix is readily absorbed and disappears within 24 hours.

331
00:44:13,624 --> 00:44:18,061
The first bath is postponed until the neonate’s temperature is stable.

332
00:44:18,061 --> 00:44:22,399
Aseptic precautions are observed in the immediate care of the cord.

333
00:44:22,399 --> 00:44:30,436
The American Academy of Pediatrics (2017a) has concluded that keeping the cord dry is sufficient care.

334
00:44:30,436 --> 00:44:36,024
The umbilical cord begins to lose water from its Wharton jelly shortly after birth.

335
00:44:36,024 --> 00:44:44,374
Within 24 hours, the cord stump loses its characteristic bluish-white, moist appearance and soon becomes dry and black.

336
00:44:44,374 --> 00:44:52,611
Within several days to weeks, the stump sloughs and leaves a small, granulating wound, which after healing forms the umbilicus.

337
00:44:52,611 --> 00:44:56,724
Separation usually takes place within the first 2 weeks.

338
00:44:56,724 --> 00:45:04,499
The umbilical cord dries more quickly and separates more readily when exposed to air (López-Medina, 2020).

339
00:45:04,499 --> 00:45:07,624
Thus, a dressing is not recommended.

340
00:45:07,624 --> 00:45:13,049
In resource-poor countries, local antimicrobial prophylaxis is reasonable .

341
00:45:13,049 --> 00:45:23,711
The World Health Organization (2014) recommends cleansing with chlorhexidine in situations in which hygienic conditions are poor or infection rates are high.

342
00:45:23,711 --> 00:45:29,974
Despite precautions, a serious umbilical infection—omphalitis— sometimes develops.

343
00:45:29,974 --> 00:45:39,686
The most likely offending organisms are Staphylococcus aureus, group A and B streptococcus, and gram-negative rods such as Escherichia coli .

344
00:45:39,686 --> 00:45:45,024
Typical signs of cellulitis and stump discharge usually aid diagnosis.

345
00:45:45,024 --> 00:45:53,111
Mild erythema and some bleeding at the stump site with cord detachment is common, but some cases present with no outward signs.

346
00:45:53,111 --> 00:45:55,786
# Feeding and Weight Loss.

347
00:45:55,786 --> 00:46:00,549
Exclusive breastfeeding is preferred for the newborn’s first 6 months.

348
00:46:00,549 --> 00:46:11,024
In 2020, 84 percent of U.S. newborns were initially breastfed, 58 percent were still nursing at 6 months, and 35 percent at 1 year .

349
00:46:11,024 --> 00:46:15,399
In many hospitals, breastfeeding begins in the delivery room.

350
00:46:15,399 --> 00:46:22,636
Most term newborns thrive best when fed 8 to 12 times daily for approximately 15 minutes each episode.

351
00:46:22,636 --> 00:46:27,724
Preterm or growth-restricted newborns require feedings at shorter intervals.

352
00:46:27,724 --> 00:46:33,236
Breastfeeding is discussed further in Chapter 36 (p. 639).

353
00:46:33,236 --> 00:46:44,811
Because most neonates actually receive little nutriment for the first 3 or 4 days of life, they progressively lose weight until the flow of maternal milk is established or other feeding is instituted.

354
00:46:44,811 --> 00:46:50,186
Preterm neonates lose relatively more weight and regain their birthweight more slowly.

355
00:46:50,186 --> 00:46:57,911
Conversely, growthrestricted but otherwise healthy newborns regain their initial weight more quickly than those born preterm.

356
00:46:57,911 --> 00:47:03,824
With proper nourishment, birthweight of term newborns usually is regained by 10 days.

357
00:47:03,824 --> 00:47:06,349
# Stools and Urine.

358
00:47:06,349 --> 00:47:12,449
For the first 2 or 3 days after birth, the colon contains soft, brown-green meconium.

359
00:47:12,449 --> 00:47:24,061
This consists of desquamated epithelial cells from the intestinal tract, mucus, epidermal cells, and lanugo (fetal hair) that have been swallowed along with amnionic fluid.

360
00:47:24,061 --> 00:47:27,824
The characteristic color results from bile pigments.

361
00:47:27,824 --> 00:47:36,911
During fetal life and for a few hours after birth, the intestinal contents are sterile, but bacteria quickly colonize the bowel contents.

362
00:47:36,911 --> 00:47:45,236
Meconium stooling is seen in 90 percent of newborns within the first 24 hours and in most of the rest within 36 hours.

363
00:47:45,236 --> 00:47:50,861
Usually, newborns first void shortly after birth but may not until the second day.

364
00:47:50,861 --> 00:47:58,111
Meconium and urine passage indicates patency of the gastrointestinal and urinary tracts, respectively.

365
00:47:58,111 --> 00:48:05,561
Failure of the newborn to stool or urinate after these times suggests a congenital defect, such as Hirschsprung disease,

366
00:48:05,716 --> 00:48:09,666
imperforate anus, or posterior urethral valve.

367
00:48:09,616 --> 00:48:18,478
After the third or fourth day, as a result of milk ingestion, meconium is replaced by light-yellow, softer, homogeneous feces.

368
00:48:18,478 --> 00:48:21,891
# Neonatal Hyperbilirubinemia.

369
00:48:21,891 --> 00:48:29,541
Between the second and fifth day of life approximately one third of all neonates develop physiological jaundice of the newborn.

370
00:48:29,541 --> 00:48:35,391
It has special significance considering most hospitals have policies for early discharge.

371
00:48:35,391 --> 00:48:45,966
Guidelines regarding standard phototherapy equipment and monitoring, as well as treatment recommendations per gestational age, hour of life, and risk factors are used .

372
00:48:45,966 --> 00:48:52,041
Hyperbilirubinemia is discussed further in Chapter 33 (p. 606).

373
00:48:52,041 --> 00:48:54,703
# Male Circumcision.

374
00:48:54,703 --> 00:49:03,216
Indications Neonatal circumcision of male newborns has been a controversial topic in the United States for at least 35 years.

375
00:49:03,216 --> 00:49:12,691
Even so, scientific evidence supports several medical benefits that include prevention of phimosis, paraphimosis, and balanoposthitis.

376
00:49:12,691 --> 00:49:19,903
Circumcision also lowers the incidence of penile cancer and of cervical cancer among their sexual partners.

377
00:49:19,903 --> 00:49:32,291
The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (2011) estimates that the newborn male circumcision rate has declined over the past decades in the United States, and rates vary widely by region.

378
00:49:32,291 --> 00:49:41,766
From a national database, the overall circumcision rate decreased from 57 percent in 2003 to 52 percent in 2016 .

379
00:49:41,766 --> 00:49:52,228
The American Academy of Pediatrics Task Force on Circumcision (2012) concluded that health benefits of newborn male circumcision outweigh the risks.

380
00:49:52,228 --> 00:50:03,103
Specific benefits include prevention of urinary tract infections, penile cancer, and transmission of some sexually transmitted infections, including HIV .

381
00:50:03,103 --> 00:50:08,003
Thus, access to the procedure is justified for families who choose it.

382
00:50:08,003 --> 00:50:12,941
The Task Force stopped short of recommending circumcision for all newborns.

383
00:50:12,941 --> 00:50:17,791
Surgical Technique Circumcision is performed only in a healthy neonate.

384
00:50:17,791 --> 00:50:27,428
Other contraindications include any genital abnormalities such as hypospadias and a family history of a bleeding disorder, unless excluded in the newborn.

385
00:50:27,428 --> 00:50:32,716
The Task Force (2012) recommends procedural analgesia.

386
00:50:32,716 --> 00:50:42,641
Various pain relief techniques include lidocaine-prilocaine topical cream, local analgesia infiltration, dorsal penile nerve block, or ring block.

387
00:50:42,641 --> 00:50:48,328
The dorsal penile nerve block or the ring block is superior to topical analgesia.

388
00:50:48,328 --> 00:50:53,678
The use of a pacifier dipped in sucrose is a useful adjunct to these methods .

389
00:50:53,678 --> 00:51:05,453
After appropriate penile cleansing, the ring block places a wheal of 1percent lidocaine at the base of the penis and then advances the needle in a 180-degree arc around the base of the penis.

390
00:51:05,453 --> 00:51:12,328
The needle is advanced first to one side and then to the other to achieve a circumferential ring of analgesia.

391
00:51:12,328 --> 00:51:15,891
The maximum dose of lidocaine is 1.0 mL.

392
00:51:15,891 --> 00:51:23,153
No vasoactive compounds such as epinephrine should ever be added to a local analgesic agent for circumcision.

393
00:51:23,153 --> 00:51:31,316
The most commonly used instruments are shown in Figure 32-5 and are Gomco and Mogen clamps and the Plastibell device.

394
00:51:31,316 --> 00:51:43,003
Compared with the Gomco procedure, Kaufman and colleagues (2002) reported that the Mogen technique required less time to perform and was associated with less apparent discomfort for the newborn.

395
00:51:43,003 --> 00:51:53,166
Regardless of the method used, the goal is to remove enough shaft skin and inner preputial epithelium so that the glans is exposed sufficiently to prevent phimosis.

396
00:51:53,166 --> 00:52:18,703
Suitable technique ensures that: (1) the amount of external skin to be removed must be accurately estimated, (2) the preputial orifice must be dilated to visualize the glans and ensure that it is normal, (3) the inner preputial epithelium must be freed from the glans epithelium, and (4) the circumcision device must be left in place long enough to produce hemostasis before excising the prepuce .

397
00:52:18,703 --> 00:52:21,403
Figure 32-5.

398
00:52:21,403 --> 00:52:24,603
Three different tools used for circumcision.

399
00:52:24,603 --> 00:52:26,691
A. Mogen clamp.

400
00:52:26,691 --> 00:52:31,128
The arms of the clamp open to a 3-mm maximum width.

401
00:52:31,128 --> 00:52:33,966
B. Gomco clamp, assembled.

402
00:52:33,966 --> 00:52:36,478
C. Plastibell device.

403
00:52:36,478 --> 00:52:41,428
The risks for bleeding, infection, and hematoma formation are low.

404
00:52:41,428 --> 00:52:50,403
Unusual complications include distal glans amputation, acquisition of HIV infection or other sexually transmitted infection,

405
00:52:50,572 --> 00:53:12,184
meatal stenosis, penile denudation, penile destruction with electrosurgical coagulation, subsequent epidermal inclusion cyst and urethrocutaneous fistula, and ischemia following the inappropriate use of lidocaine with epinephrine (El Bcheraoui, 2014; Pippi-Salle, 2013; Omole, 2020).

406
00:53:12,184 --> 00:53:15,659
# Rooming In and Hospital Discharge.

407
00:53:15,659 --> 00:53:21,197
Hospital rooming in places newborns in their mothers’ rooms instead of central nurseries.

408
00:53:21,197 --> 00:53:26,409
This practice attempts to make all phases of childbearing as natural as possible.

409
00:53:26,409 --> 00:53:32,959
It aims to foster early mother-child relationships and establish effective breast feeding practices.

410
00:53:32,959 --> 00:53:37,522
By 24 hours, the mother is generally fully ambulatory.

411
00:53:37,522 --> 00:53:42,622
Thereafter, she can usually provide routine care for herself and her newborn.

412
00:53:42,622 --> 00:53:50,547
Traditionally, the newborn is discharged with its mother, and in most cases, maternal stay has determined that of the neonate.

413
00:53:50,547 --> 00:54:01,034
From 1970 to the mid-1990s, average maternal postpartum length of stay declined steadily, and many mothers were discharged before 48 hours.

414
00:54:01,034 --> 00:54:07,734
The World Health Organization (2014) cites a minimal stay of only 24 hours.

415
00:54:07,734 --> 00:54:15,109
Although it is clear that most newborns can be safely discharged within 48 hours, this is not uniformly true.

416
00:54:15,109 --> 00:54:23,784
When Belgium recently reduced postpartum stays by 1 day, rates of neonatal readmission for jaundice and dehydration increased .

417
00:54:23,784 --> 00:54:37,897
Using Washington state data, Malkin and coworkers (2000) found that the 28-day mortality rate was increased fourfold in newborns discharged within 30 hours of birth, and the 1-year mortality rate grew twofold.

418
00:54:37,897 --> 00:54:42,459
Safe discharge for late-preterm newborns has special concerns .

419
00:54:42,459 --> 00:55:01,522
Because of the increased scrutiny regarding short hospital stays, federal legislation—The Newborns’ and Mothers’ Health Protection Act of 1996— was enacted to prohibit insurers from restricting hospital stays for mothers and newborns to less than 2 days for vaginal delivery or 4 days for cesarean delivery.

420
00:55:01,522 --> 00:55:19,097
In an analysis of more than 662,000 births in California, Datar and Sood (2006) found that readmission rates declined by 9, 12, and 20 percent, respectively, at 1, 2, and 3 years after the legislation was implemented.

